<h2>Dental crowns</h2>
<p>A crown is a custom-made cap that covers an entire tooth above the gumline. We recommend crowns when a tooth is too broken or weak for a filling, needs full coverage to protect what's left of the tooth, or has a large old filling that's failing.</p>

<h3>Modern crown materials</h3>
<p>We primarily use full-ceramic crowns &mdash; strong, natural-looking, and metal-free. For back teeth that take heavy bite force, we may recommend zirconia for extra durability.</p>

<h2>Dental bridges</h2>
<p>A bridge replaces one or more missing teeth by anchoring an artificial tooth (or teeth) to the natural teeth on either side. Bridges are a good fit when implants aren't an option, or when the neighboring teeth already need crowns anyway.</p>

<h3>Bridges vs. implants</h3>
<p>A single implant is usually a better long-term choice than a traditional bridge because it doesn't require grinding down healthy neighboring teeth. We'll be honest about which is right for you — see our <a href="/services/dental-implants/">dental implants page</a> for a comparison.</p>

<h2>What to expect</h2>
<ul class="checklist">
  <li>First visit: tooth preparation, digital scan, and a temporary crown or bridge.</li>
  <li>Lab time: 1–3 weeks while the final restoration is hand-finished.</li>
  <li>Second visit: try-in, fit check, and final cementation.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Signs you may need a crown</h2>
<ul class="checklist">
  <li>A cracked or broken tooth, or pain when you bite down and release</li>
  <li>A large old filling that is wearing out or has decay around it</li>
  <li>A back tooth that has had a root canal and needs protection from cracking</li>
  <li>Teeth worn down from grinding</li>
  <li>A badly discolored or misshapen tooth you want to fix for good</li>
</ul>

<h2>Implant crowns and implant bridges</h2>
<p>When a tooth is missing, Dr. Phillips can place the implant and restore it with the crown in the same Tempe office, so you don't bounce between a surgeon and a restoring dentist. Several missing teeth in a row can be replaced with an implant-supported bridge on two implants. See <a href="/services/dental-implants/">dental implants</a> to learn how that works.</p>

<h2>Caring for a crown or bridge</h2>
<p>Brush twice a day and floss once a day, paying attention to where the crown meets the gum. Under a bridge, use a floss threader or water flosser to clean beneath the false tooth. Avoid chewing ice and hard candy, and wear a night guard if you grind. We check every crown and bridge at your regular <a href="/services/preventive-care/">preventive visits</a>.</p>

<h2>Cost, insurance and financing</h2>
<p>The fee depends on the material and whether a build-up is needed first. We accept most major PPO plans, check your benefits before treatment, and give you a written estimate. Cherry financing and in-house payment plans can spread the cost out. See <a href="/insurance-financing/">insurance and financing</a>.</p>

